{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2216 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "a59e5e216151df2a56743ed7e4395733150abfa8f501078592f281c36dc95dbd" :edb/db-before-t 2215 :edb/db-after-t 2216 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535528 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:51:05.192Z" 13194139535528 true] [17592186179601 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353734 13194139535528 true] [17592186179601 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353741 13194139535528 true] [17592186179647 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353727 13194139535528 true] [17592186179647 1020 #edb/ref 17592186353748 13194139535528 true] [17592186179899 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535528 true] [17592186179899 1100 "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x, y) = xe^{y}$ along the quarter-circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 16$, traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4, 0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" 13194139535528 false] [17592186179899 1100 "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ traversed from the point $(0,-4)$ to the point $(4,0)$ in the fourth quadrant?" 13194139535528 true] [17592186179899 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066920/assets/0baaafbe9846ccef2c06a3b1a86eccac394344afc2dfd94bba2907ebb1cf2362.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot 4\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,{e}^{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353727 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353728 13194139535528 true] [17592186353727 1089 "q-122576" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353727 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535528 true] [17592186353727 1100 "Evaluate the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt[3]{y+1}$ along the circle $C$ with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=36.$" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353727 1113 #uuid "f7752cbf-08cf-5d87-a957-774e73db29fc" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353727 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a circle of radius $6$ centered at $O.$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36{sin}^{2}⁡t+36{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x\\sqrt[3]{y+1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}\\cdot 6\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1,$ $\\text{d}u=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt[3]{6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =6{\\int }_{1}^{1}\\sqrt[3]{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =6(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353729 13194139535528 true] [17592186353728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353730 13194139535528 true] [17592186353728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353731 13194139535528 true] [17592186353728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353732 13194139535528 true] [17592186353728 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353733 13194139535528 true] [17592186353728 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535528 true] [17592186353728 1041 "selection" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353728 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353733 13194139535528 true] [17592186353728 1110 #uuid "651a1703-0c2f-55f2-8943-f99e23f62f98" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353729 1034 "6\\sqrt[3]{2}" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353729 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535528 true] [17592186353729 1111 #uuid "6b540412-fb2a-5b6a-b348-c7da441e4824" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353730 1034 "6\\sqrt{2}" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353730 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535528 true] [17592186353730 1111 #uuid "ee828b3e-e473-596e-85f7-3e0fd1af502e" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353731 1034 "3" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353731 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535528 true] [17592186353731 1111 #uuid "5f974ac2-483f-5772-b9f5-eaf5c71f278e" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353732 1034 "6" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353732 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535528 true] [17592186353732 1111 #uuid "ef53b03d-aeed-571f-be0a-8c3ae73d4c2e" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353733 1034 "0" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353733 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535528 true] [17592186353733 1111 #uuid "1b340d24-d37d-51e7-9982-4014c14fff51" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353734 1017 #edb/ref 17592186353735 13194139535528 true] [17592186353734 1089 "q-122586" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353734 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535528 true] [17592186353734 1100 "Which of the following gives the line integral of the function $f(x,y)=y\\,\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}$ along the quarter-circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant?" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353734 1113 #uuid "7aa8bcd7-fed3-5186-b1c1-b187da2c7965" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353734 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14066920/assets/d0ae53dd5d0932cbf834a166e851c227f75cb546b591a9fd43930ec06f0e9ddf.png)\n\nThe curve $C$ can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y\\,\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{4-(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{4-4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}y\\,\\sqrt{4-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }4{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353736 13194139535528 true] [17592186353735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353737 13194139535528 true] [17592186353735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353738 13194139535528 true] [17592186353735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353739 13194139535528 true] [17592186353735 1000 #edb/ref 17592186353740 13194139535528 true] [17592186353735 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535528 true] [17592186353735 1041 "selection" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353735 1104 #edb/ref 17592186353740 13194139535528 true] [17592186353735 1110 #uuid "ed03dcbc-c76f-58e5-8b96-ed8d21754b75" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353736 1034 "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" 13194139535528 true] [17592186353736 1103 #edb/ref 